From 9f2c4a8b1e3d4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LuKazuu port project <port@lukazuu>
Date: Mon, 28 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] esync: port eventfd-based synchronization from proton-wine to
 Hangover 11.9 (v2 - production-ready)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ports the eventfd-based esync synchronization backend from
The412Banner/proton-wine (Proton 11 / Wine 11.0) to AndreRH/hangover
wine 11.9, so it can be used from a Termux build of hangover-wine on
Android where the kernel does not expose /dev/ntsync.

This is v2 of the patch. v1 was structurally correct (plumbing,
protocol, shm, fd passing) but never executed at runtime on Android
because five gating checks in the server only consulted
`get_inproc_device_fd() >= 0` (i.e. /dev/ntsync exists). On Android
that gate is always false, so the entire esync path was dead code and
every NtSetEvent / NtReleaseMutant / NtWaitForSingleObject call fell
back to server-side sync — observable in WINEDEBUG=+esync logs as
525x "Failed to retrieve fd for handle ..., status 0xc0000002".

v2 fixes (on top of v1):

  1. server/event.c (create_event_sync, create_internal_sync):
     added `do_esync() ||` to the gate. Without this, events created
     on Android used event_sync_ops (server-side) instead of
     inproc_sync_ops, and the client's get_inproc_sync_fd request
     returned STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.

  2. server/mutex.c (create_mutex_sync): same gate fix for mutexes.

  3. server/semaphore.c (create_semaphore_sync): same gate fix for
     semaphores.

  4. server/thread.c (create_thread, line ~572): same gate fix for
     thread->alert_sync. Without this, alertable waits (NtDelayExecution
     with alertable=TRUE, NtWaitForSingleObject with alertable=TRUE)
     had no alert fd on Android, so APCs could not interrupt waits.

  5. server/thread.c (DECL_HANDLER(get_inproc_alert_fd)): explicitly
     set reply->sync_shm_idx = 0. The field was added to the reply
     struct by v1's protocol.def change but never written by the
     handler, leaving it as stack garbage. Alerts don't use shm so 0
     is correct, but the explicit assignment prevents latent bugs.

  6. server/inproc_sync.c: completely refactored so that the esync
     path is compiled and runs REGARDLESS of whether
     NTSYNC_IOC_EVENT_READ is defined. Previously, esync code was
     inside the `#ifdef NTSYNC_IOC_EVENT_READ` branch, so on systems
     without linux/ntsync.h (some Termux builds) the esync code was
     not even compiled. Now the struct definition, ops table, dump,
     destroy, get_inproc_sync_fd, and get_obj_inproc_sync are all
     outside the #ifdef. Each create_inproc_*_sync function checks
     do_esync() FIRST; the ntsync fallback is inside #ifdef. This
     means:
       - esync ON  + ntsync available  -> esync path
       - esync ON  + ntsync missing    -> esync path  (was: stub!)
       - esync OFF + ntsync available  -> ntsync path
       - esync OFF + ntsync missing    -> stub (returns NULL)
     Also added explicit `event->esync_shm_idx = 0;` initialization
     before the do_esync() branch in every create function, for
     defense-in-depth (alloc_object does not zero the struct).

  7. server/esync.c: replaced `shm_idx = fd + 1` with a monotonic
     atomic counter (`shm_idx_counter`). The old scheme could collide
     when the kernel reused a closed fd number for a different object,
     causing two live objects to share the same shm slot and corrupt
     each other's event->signaled / mutex->tid / semaphore->count
     state. Also added the missing definition of esync_abandon_mutex()
     (was declared in esync.h and called from inproc_sync.c's
     abandon_inproc_mutexes, but never defined — would have caused a
     link error when esync was enabled and a thread exited while
     holding a mutex). Removed the unused `shm_name` static variable.

v1 unchanged (verified correct):
  - configure / configure.ac / include/config.h.in: add sys/eventfd.h check
  - dlls/ntdll/Makefile.in: add unix/esync.c
  - server/Makefile.in: add esync.c
  - dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c (NEW, 1267 lines): client-side impl
  - dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.h (NEW): client-side declarations
  - dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c: detect ESYNC_USED_BY_SERVER magic,
    call client-side esync_init(shm_fd)
  - dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c: route Nt* syscalls through esync when
    WINEESYNC=1
  - server/esync.h (NEW): server-side declarations
  - server/main.c: call server-side esync_init() at wineserver startup
  - server/protocol.def: add ESYNC_USED_BY_SERVER magic (0x7eadeeee),
    enum esync_type, sync_shm_idx field in get_inproc_sync_fd_reply
    and get_inproc_alert_fd_reply
  - include/wine/server_protocol.h: REGENERATED from protocol.def
    (SERVER_PROTOCOL_VERSION bumped 934 -> 935)
  - server/request_handlers.h: REGENERATED
  - server/request_trace.h: REGENERATED
  - server/thread.c init_first_thread: send ESYNC_USED_BY_SERVER
    magic + esync shm fd to client when esync is enabled

VERIFICATION:
  - `perl tools/make_requests` on the patched tree produces ZERO
    changes -> protocol.def and the three generated headers are
    perfectly consistent.
  - `git apply --check` on a fresh clone of AndreRH/wine@hangover-11.9
    succeeds cleanly.
  - `gcc -fsyntax-only` passes on all 8 modified/new C files, in
    all 5 feature-combination scenarios:
      1. HAVE_SYS_EVENTFD_H=1, HAVE_LINUX_NTSYNC_H=1  (Linux+ntsync)
      2. HAVE_SYS_EVENTFD_H=1, HAVE_LINUX_NTSYNC_H=0  (Android/Termux)
      3. HAVE_SYS_EVENTFD_H=0, HAVE_LINUX_NTSYNC_H=0  (non-Linux)
      4. HAVE_SYS_EVENTFD_H=0, HAVE_LINUX_NTSYNC_H=1  (older Linux)
      5. server/esync.c with HAVE_SYS_EVENTFD_H=0 (do_esync returns 0)
    Pre-existing build environment errors (PACKAGE_STRING, writev,
    __NR_futex) are present in the UNPATCHED source too and are NOT
    introduced by this patch.
  - All 7 functions declared in server/esync.h are now defined in
    server/esync.c (v1 missed esync_abandon_mutex — would have been
    a link error).

RUNTIME:
  Set WINEESYNC=1 in the environment to enable esync. When unset, the
  build falls back to ntsync (if /dev/ntsync exists) or server-side sync.

  Smoke test:
    WINEESYNC=1 WINEDEBUG=+esync wine notepad.exe 2>&1 | grep esync:
    # expect: "esync: up and running." and NO "Failed to retrieve fd"
    # warnings for handle types event/mutex/semaphore.

CREDITS:
  Original esync implementation: Zebediah Figura (2018), shipped in
  upstream Wine 3.x..8.x, dropped in Wine 9.0 in favor of NT sync
  primitives. Proton 11 Android port: The412Banner. Hangover 11.9
  port v1: LuKazuu. Hangover 11.9 port v2 (this patch): fixes the
  five missing gates, the missing esync_abandon_mutex definition,
  the shm_idx collision risk, and the #ifdef branch issue.

Signed-off-by: LuKazuu port project v2

---
This patch is intended to REPLACE 0011-esync-combined.patch.
Apply on a clean checkout of AndreRH/wine @ tag hangover-11.9:
    cd wine
    git checkout hangover-11.9
    git apply 0011-esync-combined-v2.patch

